  "body": "The maintainer asked for this in chat on 2026-11-15, with the DXF work of #1452.\n\nHis words: \"A DXF should be possible to \"place\" on a stock and used as references for cutting - not be treated like a model - thus for processes like wireedm, plasma, laser, etc etc.. we can just make a sheet, cube, cylinder. place the DXF - and select operations accordingly.\"\n\nWhat is missing: the sub-issues of #1452 give DXF sketches to the milling operations (#1864, #1867, #1868, #1869) and to the plasma profile (#1870). No issue gives them to Wire EDM or to a laser cut.\n\nDepends on #1861 and #1862 (the `Sketch` entity placed on a stock, with the virtual model reference).\n\nScope:\n\n1. Wire EDM 2-axis profile on DXF sketch chains: the user picks closed or open chains, and the side and the direction for each chain. The kerf (wire radius and spark gap) offset uses the same law as the model-based Wire EDM operation.\n2. The stock gives the top and the bottom of the cut. No solid model is necessary.\n3. Laser: find out if a laser cut operation exists. If it exists, give it the same chain picking. If it does not exist, say so in a comment and file the issue for it. Do not add a laser operation in this issue.\n4. The kerf verify law runs against the sketch chains. An offset that cannot fit (a slot narrower than the kerf, a self-crossing offset) refuses by name. It never posts a path that cuts on the wrong side.\n5. The commands have their MCP tools, or a follow-up issue `MCP: <command>`.\n\nThis changes posted G-code (new programs). It is under the safety floor.\n\nDone means:\n\n- A setup with a sheet stock and a placed DXF profile posts a Wire EDM 2-axis program for a closed chain and for an open chain.\n- Golden CL and posted goldens pin the side, the direction and the kerf offset.\n- The refusals of item 4 are pinned.\n- The PR body says what was not exercised on a real control.\n",
